# Accepted memcheck reports for the taint harness. Each entry is a
# secret-dependent access or branch that the design documents and accepts;
# scripts/ct-taint.sh fails on anything not listed here. Add an entry only
# with a reason. Frame names are demangled Rust symbols, matched as globs.
#
# Every mode (src/main.rs, run by scripts/ct-taint.sh) encrypts a sealed
# plaintext through `OreScheme::encrypt_plaintext` or
# `encrypt_query_plaintext`: vitaminc encodes the value untainted, and the
# harness marks the plaintext's bytes undefined in place. The Bit6 modes take
# a u64 (full and left), an i64, an f64 and a u32. The chained modes take text
# under the default padding (full and left), and a u128 and bytes, which go in
# unpadded. Both engines get their symbols from the 6-bit decomposition, which
# masks rather than checks, and `permute` takes an in-domain `Symbol`, so the
# Bit6 modes and the unpadded chained modes report nothing.
#
# Two reports are accepted. The first is `Blocks6::check`, which no mode
# reaches now: it guards the raw-block entry points (Bit6's crate-private
# `encrypt`/`encrypt_left` over 6-bit symbols, and the chained scheme's
# test-only `encrypt_var`), which only the crate's own tests call. The entry
# is kept for a mode that is added on that path. A caller of those passes
# symbols that may be out of the 6-bit domain, and the function must refuse
# them. It ORs every symbol's high bits together without branching and
# branches once on the result, so the branch reveals only whether some symbol
# was out of domain. For a well-formed input it always goes the same way.
# `check` is `#[inline(never)]`, so the branch is always in its own frame,
# whose name is `ore_rs::scheme::decompose::Blocks6<B>::check` (or its
# v0-mangled form, which also contains `Blocks6` and `check`).

{
   blocks6-check: one branch on whether any raw symbol is out of domain
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*Blocks6*check*
}

# The second is `refuse_trailing_nul`, on the chained text path under a
# padding policy, which the text modes use (the default); the u128 and bytes
# modes are unpadded and never reach it. Padded with NUL bytes, text that ends
# in NUL would encrypt the same as the text without it, so it must be refused.
# The function branches once, on whether the last byte is NUL, and returns a
# constant on each path, so the branch reveals only whether the encryption is
# refused. It is `#[inline(never)]`, and its frame is
# `ore_rs::scheme::chained::refuse_trailing_nul`.

{
   chained-trailing-nul: one branch on whether a padded string ends in NUL
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*chained*refuse_trailing_nul*
}
